// The short-column read every per-parameter bit column is asked through, so "recorded nothing here"
// has one spelling.
function bitAt(column BoolArray, index ParamCount) returns bool
	if index >= column.count() as ParamCount 'past'
		return false
	end 'past'

	return try column.get(index) otherwise panic("bitAt: column.get OOB at {index} despite index < count")
end 'bitAt'

function forwardedStoreIsTakenBy(callee ParamConsumeInfo, column ParamCount, label ByteArray) returns bool
	let position = try argSlotPosition(callee.paramNames, argIndex: column, label: label) otherwise return false
	return callee.takesPromiseAt(position) or bitAt(callee.handedOn, index: position)
end 'forwardedStoreIsTakenBy'

// ⭐⭐ **RECORD ONE PARAMETER'S BIT IN A SWEEP COLUMN, GROWING THE COLUMN TO REACH IT** — the ONE writer of
// the sweep's bit columns (consume and hand-on among them), paired with the ONE reader every consumer
// goes through (`bitAt` / `ParamConsumeInfo.isConsumed`). A column reaches only as far as the
// highest position that has a fact, which is exactly what those readers already mean by a SHORT column:
// *"a position past either column is a parameter the sweep recorded nothing for"*. So a pre-fill would be
// writing out, per parameter of every declaration in the program, the very default its readers supply free.
//
// ⛔⛔ **THE COLUMNS START FROM A FRESH ARRAY AND NEVER FROM A SHARED MODULE-LEVEL EMPTY ONE.** The sweep WRITES
// them, and a shared empty handed to a name it then writes is a `let` global's record handed to a name that is
// written through: the rule that a value which may alias a `let` global's record may not be written through
// refuses that (E3207). A column that is always fresh can alias nothing, and costs one empty record.
function setParamBit(column BoolArray, index ParamCount)
	while column.count() as ParamCount <= index 'reach'
		column.push(false)
	end 'reach'

	try column.set(index, value: true) otherwise panic("setParamBit: column.set OOB at {index} despite the growth above reaching it")
end 'setParamBit'

// `setParamBit`'s twin for the type-parameter FEED column, read under the same rule —
// `ParamConsumeInfo.typeParamFeedOf` answers `none` for a position past the end, *"exactly as `isConsumed`
// answers `false` for its short `bits`"*. Growing on demand buys more here than it does for a bit:
// `TypeParamFeed.none` is a UNION value, so a pre-fill would BOX one per parameter of every method of
// every generic type in the program purely to say "nothing here".
function setParamFeed(column TypeParamFeedArray, index ParamCount, feed TypeParamFeed)
	while column.count() as ParamCount <= index 'reach'
		column.push(TypeParamFeed.none)
	end 'reach'

	try column.set(index, value: feed) otherwise panic("setParamFeed: column.set OOB at {index} despite the growth above reaching it")
end 'setParamFeed'

// Is any bit of `column` set? The shared body of `ParamConsumeInfo`'s whole-column questions, so "this function
// states something" cannot come to read the columns differently.
function anyBitSet(column BoolArray) returns bool
	for bit in column 'each'
		if bit 'set'
			return true
		end 'set'
	end 'each'

	return false
end 'anyBitSet'

// ⭐⭐ **MAY A CALL TO THIS FUNCTION HAND BACK A `let`-DECLARED TOP-LEVEL GLOBAL'S OWN RECORD?** —
// the whole-program fact that lets the immutability rule be enforced AT THE WRITE (⚖ user, 2026-08-14)
// rather than at the return. Reading a `let` global and returning it is legal; MUTATING through what came
// back is the error, and the caller can only know to refuse that if it knows what the callee's return may
// alias. The callee's body is routinely in another file, so no per-file parse can answer it.
//
// ⭐ **A PER-DECLARATION FACT DERIVED FROM BODIES BY THE TOKEN SWEEP, WHOSE TRANSITIVE HALF NO SINGLE FILE
// CAN SETTLE** — recorded raw here and closed by `ProgramSignatures.closeGlobalAliasReturns` once every file
// is in, over `DeclNodeSpace` and the `ForwardCall` edge shape.
//
// ⭐⭐⭐ **THE SWEEP IS DELIBERATELY NARROW, AND WHAT MAKES THAT SOUND IS A FALLBACK RATHER THAN COVERAGE.**
// A `return` this scan does not recognise records nothing, so the function's bit stays clear — and a bit
// that is clear is exactly the condition under which `Parser.ownedFormOfBorrowedValue` keeps the ORIGINAL
// E2015 refusal at the return. So:
//
//   • **UNDER-approximating (missing a seed or an edge) costs a REFUSAL, never a silent acceptance.** The
//     program is rejected with the ORIGINAL E2015 message.
//   • **OVER-approximating (a spurious seed or edge) costs a FALSE REJECT at some later write**, which is
//     the only real hazard here.
//
